w24-threshold-compliance
------------------------

This directory is a clean-room Python 3 standard-library method sketch. It is
original work for this repository and contains no third-party code, data,
prompts, items, labels, figures or weights.

Paper (method idea credited)
----------------------------
Dongsun Moon.
"Abstention Is Not Threshold Compliance: Stated Scoring Rules in Open
Language Models".
arXiv:2611.14661, v1, submitted 10 Nov 2026. Primary category cs.CL,
cross-listed cs.AI.
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14661
Paper licence: CC BY 4.0, http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ (as
linked on the abs page). Only the abstract is quoted or used; the PDF was
not read.

Code and data
-------------
The abstract and comments link no code or data. Nothing was downloaded,
cloned, installed or run.

Method only: the idea that a stated scoring rule with threshold t asks a
model to answer only when its probability of being correct exceeds t, that
a high abstention rate alone is not evidence of compliance, that decisions
no policy with threshold-independent beliefs could make can be detected
from decisions alone, and that evaluations should report the abstention
rate, the response to the threshold and the answered items. The declared
thresholds, the tie rule, the two audit modes, the rate-only baseline, the
oracle and the fixtures (items, caller-labelled probabilities and
policies) are this sketch's own. No text, figures, tables or data from the
paper are copied into the code; no model was run. The paper's claims appear
only in paper_evidence.json, quoted and marked UNVERIFIED.
